WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.290
CHAD JENNINGS: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:08.440
My name is Chad, and
thanks for joining us.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.750
And today we're going to
talk about reinventing

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:13.900
geospatial analytics,
and I'm joined today

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.510
by Joe Bettridge from TELUS.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:19.630
And the key point
of this talk is

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:24.340
going to be to describe what
becomes available to users

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.970
and customers and their users
when you really take the brakes

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:31.178
off geospatial analytics.

00:00:31.178 --> 00:00:32.220
JOE BETTRIDGE: Hi, there.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.180
I'm Joe Bettridge.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.830
I lead the TELUS Insights
consulting program at TELUS.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.200
We are a heavy user
of Google BigQuery

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.680
as well as BigQuery GIS.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:43.680
CHAD JENNINGS: Right on.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.830
And Joe and I are both
excited to show you

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.420
what we've been up to.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.540
So here are the two of us.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:52.380
We're your speakers.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.060
I'm a product
manager at BigQuery,

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.662
and I'm also the GIS
lead for Google Cloud.

00:00:57.662 --> 00:00:59.620
JOE BETTRIDGE: And I am
the consulting practice

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.080
lead for TELUS Insights.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.080
CHAD JENNINGS: Right on.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.500
OK, so without further ado,
let's go ahead and get into it.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.220
We'll talk about
use cases, and Joe

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:11.860
is going to go through
some of the algorithms

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.800
that TELUS is using
inside of BigQuery GIS.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.980
But to set that up, I'm going
to go through a couple--

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:18.903
call it terms of art.

00:01:18.903 --> 00:01:21.070
We're going to go through
BigQuery just a little bit

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:24.520
and what BigQuery GIS
is, and then we're

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.300
going to get into
some more detail.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.740
So if you already are very
familiar with the product,

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:34.090
put me on 1 and 1/2 times speed
for the next minute and a half.

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:36.100
So with that, let's jump in.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.150
For those not familiar, BigQuery
is Google Cloud's enterprise

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:41.470
data warehouse.

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.740
So looking at the left
side of the screen here,

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.950
we're primarily
an analytics tool,

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.680
and the product's
real claim to fame

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:55.120
here is that we can scale
from gigabytes to petabytes

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.080
without encumbering the
user with having to manage

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:02.200
any of that infrastructure.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.270
You interact with
the product in SQL,

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.880
so it essentially looks
like a relational database

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.280
that can scale up to petabytes.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.800
Data inside of BigQuery
is encrypted, durable,

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:13.300
and the product is
highly available.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:17.200
As a matter of fact, we
just launched four nines

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.660
of availability as
our new SLA, which

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:22.630
is industry leading,
which is really cool.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:26.010
We want to make the product
as differentiated and useful

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.980
to users as possible, so we
have a couple of unique items

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.320
that I wanted to talk about.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.700
The fact that BigQuery is
fully managed and serverless,

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.320
those are the things
that enable us

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:42.870
to scale to petabytes without
making users provision hardware

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.160
and things of that nature.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:48.510
We have a real-time
streaming API

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:51.390
so you can stream data
straight into BigQuery.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.220
And then using the
BI engine, which

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:54.930
is on the bottom
of the list here,

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.250
you can actually query
that in real time.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.340
So you can do real-time
insights on data

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.020
streamed right into
your data warehouse

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.330
without having to
use any other tools.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.760
We've also invented
some new SQL verbs

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.650
so that you can do
machine learning right

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:09.820
inside the platform.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:12.720
And what this talk is all
about is some other SQL verbs.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:13.640
We didn't invent them.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:16.110
We're using the PostGIS syntax.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:21.720
But by bringing geospatial SQL
functions and geospatial data

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.960
types into BigQuery,
we're enabling

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.120
a whole new class of use
cases inside the product.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.460
So let's dive in.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.560
What is BigQuery GIS?

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.190
First and foremost, it's
supporting a new geography data

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:35.940
type.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:38.940
We launched this to alpha a
couple of years ago in 2018.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:40.290
It's since gone GA.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:44.700
So this is a very solid
offering inside the product.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.190
And a geography data
type, as you can see,

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.590
can have several
different shapes.

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:52.860
Points, line, strings,
polygons, multis, collections,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:57.120
all of these fit inside
the geography data type.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.460
To operate on those
data types, we

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.290
have a number of
different functions.

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:07.380
So we've got about 50 or
so PostGIS syntax functions

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.410
that we've implemented
inside of BigQuery,

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:14.460
and these function types
take the categories here.

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.220
And they cover the
primary and the majority

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:20.528
of the functionality that
folks use inside of PostGIS.

00:04:20.528 --> 00:04:22.320
What's really exciting
about some of these,

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.600
though, is that
particularly the predicates,

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:27.600
you can do geospatial
joins based on predicates.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.670
So if you want to
join two tables based

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.130
on whether a point
is inside a polygon,

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:35.190
that is totally within bounds.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.890
If you want to do that on
data sets that are really big,

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:40.710
like terabytes or
hundreds of terabytes,

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.860
that also is totally
within bounds.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.610
And Joe is going to
talk a little bit more

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:48.810
about what becomes possible
when those limits are

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.600
relaxed for your workloads.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:55.980
Speaking of workloads, almost
any analytics workload,

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:59.010
whether it's geospatial or
not, has these components.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:02.520
First thing, you need to
get data into your product

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.200
or into the data warehouse.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:06.810
So that's the ingest here.

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:10.420
Then you have to analyze it
inside the data warehouse.

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:14.670
And then results as a table
are interesting for some result

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:17.400
sets, I suppose, but really
you want to visualize that.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.470
And particularly
for geospatial, you

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:21.820
want to visualize
those on a map.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:24.240
And so I wanted
to call these out

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.090
because there are some aspects
here that BigQuery and BigQuery

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:29.070
GIS are really
struggling at, and there

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:32.460
are some places where we need
our partner tools to really

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.090
flesh out these workflows.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:36.040
So I'm going to go through
from left to right.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:41.430
So in ingest, BigQuery has a
load API and a streaming API.

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:43.350
And so if you want to--

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:48.000
a lot of our, for example,
industrial IoT customers

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.570
are using the streaming API
to stream instrument or sensor

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:54.810
data straight into
BigQuery tables.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:56.910
We have customers
using the BigQuery load

00:05:56.910 --> 00:06:00.180
API to load as much
as a petabyte per day,

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.760
and we have customers
using the streaming API

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:08.720
to load up to 10 gigabytes
per second per table.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:12.030
So that handles data
that we would just

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:13.980
want to flow straight
in, but let's say

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:19.290
you have data sets that are in
specific geospatial formats.

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:22.260
For that, we've partnered
with Safe Software,

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:27.210
and they have written a BigQuery
emitter for their tool FME.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:32.040
This tool allows you to import
and materializes a BigQuery

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:37.260
table any number of the
hundreds of geospatial formats

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:38.440
that they support.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.700
So if you've got shapefiles
or KML or GeoJSON

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:44.910
or what have you--

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:47.250
geospatial data format X--

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:49.500
chances are FME
supports it, and you

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:52.980
can bring that straight in
using a drag-and-drop tool

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:54.870
into BigQuery.

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:57.880
So ingest, cool stuff.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:06:59.670
Now, the real
strength of BigQuery

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:02.700
shows up in this Analyze
column in that BigQuery,

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:05.430
as I mentioned before, the
strength of this product

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:12.340
is that it can process
data at the petabyte scale.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:16.240
And so when you add
geospatial analysis to this,

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:19.590
you can now process GIS
data at the petabyte scale,

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:22.240
which really hasn't
been possible before.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:24.390
And so this type
of data, I'm really

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:27.690
talking about structured
and semistructured data.

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:31.290
For imagery data, we have to
turn to tools like Google Earth

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:33.030
Engine and Descartes
Labs, and I'll

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:36.040
talk about those in a little
bit more detail later.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:37.810
So once you've
done your analysis,

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:42.280
let's move on to
serve and visualize.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:45.570
Particularly with
geospatial analyses,

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:46.860
you don't look at a table.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:48.870
You want to see your
results on a map.

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:52.440
And we have a multitude of
tools, some of them specified

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:55.650
for different personas or
different types of users

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:58.500
to put those results in
a map and then serve them

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:01.350
either as a report or
as a portal or just

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.720
as an image for an analyst.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:06.450
The ones I've
highlighted here in bold

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:09.450
are ones that have direct
connectors to BigQuery.

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:13.140
So you don't have to extract a
file and then transform a file

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:14.430
and then render it.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:18.450
So there's a healthy selection
of folks that connect directly

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:21.320
to BigQuery.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:24.930
So let's talk about a couple
of these tools in detail.

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:27.080
First off, let's talk
about Google Earth Engine

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:30.290
as it performs on
imagery analytics.

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:33.950
So Earth Engine is a
tool, a product at Google.

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:36.200
It's been around
for about 10 years,

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:39.135
and it has an immense data
catalog of satellite imagery,

00:08:39.135 --> 00:08:40.850
and it's about 30 petabytes.

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:44.270
And the thing that's really
cool about their data catalog

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:46.940
is that when new satellite
imagery comes in,

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:49.850
Earth Engine as a service
will automatically

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:52.280
register and
orthorectify that imagery

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:55.520
so that it is includable
in your analysis

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:57.030
with a single line of code.

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.510
So for example, like
#include for those

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:06.050
that speak C, #include sentinel
imagery from this date, date A.

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:07.700
And boom, that's
it, and now you've

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:10.880
got the entire globe of
sentinel imagery from that date.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:13.820
You can then include
central imagery from date B

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:17.150
to do an A/B comparison,
again with just a couple lines

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:18.350
of code.

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:20.600
Very cool stuff.

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:23.870
Descartes Labs is another
image-processing tool that

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:25.910
connects directly to BigQuery.

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:28.850
They themselves have an
immense data catalog,

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:33.890
and some of these thumbnails
here describe elements of it--

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:38.660
for example, like SAR imagery,
AIS, which is automatic--

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:40.820
actually, I've forgotten
the acronym to that,

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:43.880
but that one's actually ship
positions showing shipping

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:46.220
lanes all over the world.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:48.350
They also have a
dedicated solutions team.

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:53.010
So if there's an aspect of this
data that you want to process

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:54.980
and you need some
support, Descartes Labs

00:09:54.980 --> 00:10:00.670
has a lot of detailed
expertise to help you along.

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:03.270
So the next few slides, we're
going to step in and kind of

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:05.130
go persona by persona.

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:09.360
So analysts-- if you're
an analyst, you speak SQL.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:10.870
You're using BigQuery.

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:12.120
You look in the documentation.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:15.030
You figure out which
ST commands you need,

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:16.290
and you write your query.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:17.970
And you just want
to see those results

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:22.860
on a map to understand
what your results are.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:25.720
BigQuery Geo Viz is an
excellent tool for that.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:29.543
So you can either write
the query in the UI,

00:10:29.543 --> 00:10:31.710
and then there's a dropdown
where you can explore it

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:32.970
directly in Geo Viz.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:35.310
You can compose your query
in the Geo Viz window, which

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:37.260
then runs it against BigQuery.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:41.820
And then that allows you then
to do interactive styling

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.160
and visualization, pan and zoom.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:46.880
This is all on top
of Google Maps.

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:49.610
We've also done a
bunch of upgrades

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:53.210
to this little product
over the last few months.

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:57.830
So we've blown away the
visualization limitations.

00:10:57.830 --> 00:11:00.530
And so it's essentially that
you can render as many points

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:02.900
as your browser can hold.

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:06.600
And we've enabled the ability
to share maps with colleagues.

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:11.180
So this is not a full-featured
geospatial-analysis platform.

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:13.820
Like I said, it's a way to
get your query on a screen

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:15.870
so you can understand it.

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:20.240
But it's a handy little tool
that we like quite a lot.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:23.350
So for developers,
BigQuery GIS is also

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:25.330
supported by the
Google Maps API.

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:29.800
So you can bring BigQuery
data straight into your map

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:32.300
applications and
map visualizations.

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:34.990
So you can see here
this is actually a heat

00:11:34.990 --> 00:11:38.050
map of taxi trips in Manhattan.

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:40.930
So that center image is
the island of Manhattan.

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:43.300
And then the API has
some really nifty ways

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:45.910
to call out specific areas,
which are shown in the two

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:48.220
images on the right.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.260
Now, business users--
if you're not an analyst

00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:52.180
and you're not a
developer and you just

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:54.730
want to operate in a
BI tool, again, we've

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:55.660
got you covered here.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:12:00.080
Both Data Studio and Looker have
support for BigQuery GIS types

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:02.590
or BigQuery geography types.

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:06.460
And so you can use these tools
to create dashboards on top

00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:09.820
of pan-and-zoomable maps.

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.910
Now, one element that we
haven't touched on yet

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:16.180
that I'm very excited
to announce today right

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:21.710
now in this section
is the Carto Tiler.

00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:23.540
With BigQuery, you
have the ability

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:27.080
to process enormous
volumes of data.

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:30.980
The tools that I've shown you
before are very, very capable,

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:35.460
but they don't have the ability
to render the same level

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:39.350
and same magnitude of volumes
of data that BigQuery can.

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:42.380
And Carto, our partner,
has solved that.

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:45.110
And what they've done
with the Carto Tiler

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:47.630
is they've created
a way to create

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:52.610
a hierarchy of data that's
corresponding to zoom levels

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:53.220
here.

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:55.400
So what you're seeing
in the right image

00:12:55.400 --> 00:13:00.190
is the building locations pulled
from the OpenStreetMap data

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:02.600
set, which incidentally
is available in BigQuery

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:03.860
public data.

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:05.880
So you can create
this map as well.

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:11.040
And there are 300 million
dots worldwide on this map.

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:13.340
And so if we click
into that map,

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:16.460
you can see how fluid and
detailed the rendering

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:21.020
is as you go from street level
all the way back out to orbit.

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:24.740
And again, we're
announcing the availability

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:26.400
of Carto's beta today.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.160
There are some links over
here on the left-hand side.

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:31.250
So you can play with
this map yourself,

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:32.960
And there's an
email address there

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:37.720
for you to send a note to
to get more information.

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:41.610
So just in case you thought
the Carto Tiler only

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:45.720
worked on points, here's
an example on polygons.

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:49.710
So we're going to zoom
in here again on New York

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:54.210
where the southwest corner has
some really interesting-shaped

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:57.250
apartment buildings.

00:13:57.250 --> 00:14:00.850
So the links here are on the
slide on the bottom left,

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:05.020
and we are very excited to
announce this with our partner

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:11.620
Carto as now visualization
can approach the same scale

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:13.630
as BigQuery's native compute.

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:14.890
So very exciting stuff.

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:15.390
Thank you.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:22.980
The last section before
I hand it off to Joe

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:26.190
is I wanted to talk at a high
level about the use cases

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:30.510
that we are seeing our
customers employ BigQuery GIS.

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:33.750
So starting on the
left-hand side,

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:37.230
in retail, the primary
operations in retail that we've

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:40.500
seen are channel
optimization, site selection,

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:42.840
and marketing analysis
by trade area.

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:47.400
Because BigQuery allows you
to define your own polygons

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:51.810
obviously, you can define your
own data-driven trade areas

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:54.930
rather than using
the established ones

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:58.020
like either census blocks or
zip codes or any other kind

00:14:58.020 --> 00:14:59.280
of established polygon.

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:02.430
You can define your own that
are specific to your business's

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:05.880
needs and then run whatever
aggregations you need,

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:08.670
again because the
scale will support you.

00:15:08.670 --> 00:15:11.490
In supply chain and
logistics, we've

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:15.960
seen a number of our
customers use IoT,

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:19.620
so Internet of Things, data
to do traffic analysis, which

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:21.090
is what this image shows.

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:26.490
This is actually a heat map of
ML-predicted hazardous driving

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:30.310
areas developed by our
partner and customer Geotab.

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:32.610
So they actually
used vehicular data

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:34.560
combined with
weather data that's

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:39.000
already stored in BigQuery to
do a GIS plus machine learning

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:43.770
tool to say you can dial
in a weather condition

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:46.950
and then have that predict what
the hazardous driving would

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:49.050
be for that weather condition.

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:52.760
So very useful for city
planners and traffic planners.

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:56.120
We see a lot of activity
around delivery optimization

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:58.400
and, really gratifyingly,
we're seeing

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:02.760
some significant projects around
supply-chain sustainability.

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:07.040
So this is calculating the
human cost, the economic cost,

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:09.350
and the environmental
cost of taking product

00:16:09.350 --> 00:16:13.490
from its source, which may be
many, many thousands of miles

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:16.370
away from its ultimate
destination on the shelf

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:17.660
before you buy it.

00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:20.570
And so knowing the true
costs of that supply chain

00:16:20.570 --> 00:16:24.200
and being able to optimize
that supply chain to make it

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:26.330
economically,
environmentally sustainable

00:16:26.330 --> 00:16:28.160
is really exciting work.

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:33.380
We all know how important
public dissemination of data

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:36.980
is in this time and
age, and so we've

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:41.840
seen a number of US states
and countries around the world

00:16:41.840 --> 00:16:46.370
use BigQuery GIS and
our dashboarding tools

00:16:46.370 --> 00:16:49.490
to support public data portals.

00:16:49.490 --> 00:16:52.400
Also very interestingly,
this is an air-quality map

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:56.420
from our colleagues
at Aclima and Geotab

00:16:56.420 --> 00:16:58.910
where they're actually
discovering, one,

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:02.690
that air quality can change
on a block-by-block basis

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:08.030
and, two, that the reduction in
human travel during these COVID

00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:12.740
times has greatly reduced
the pollutants in the air.

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:15.530
And they have a lot of research
that they've used BigQuery

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:18.930
and BigQuery GIS to support.

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:21.810
And then lastly in
agriculture, we've

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:25.300
seen a lot of usage
around yield optimization,

00:17:25.300 --> 00:17:28.170
so trying to get the most
food production out of a field

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:29.657
that you possibly can.

00:17:29.657 --> 00:17:31.740
And then once you've figured
it out for the field,

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:34.240
because these tools,
BigQuery and, in this case,

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:36.870
Earth Engine operate
at such scale,

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:40.980
you can do these analyses for
the entire globe in one shot.

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:45.570
And so that way S4 Agtech,
another one of our customers,

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:49.920
is doing worldwide
production forecasting.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:53.040
Figuring out how much grain
the Ukraine is going to do

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:55.110
is just one step
of their process

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:57.510
because they can do that for
the Ukraine, for Argentina,

00:17:57.510 --> 00:18:00.710
the entire globe in one shot.

00:18:00.710 --> 00:18:03.940
So with that, let me
wrap up my section.

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:07.060
I've given you a very
high-level introduction

00:18:07.060 --> 00:18:11.110
to both BigQuery, BigQuery
GIS, and some of the partner

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:13.030
ecosystem and use cases.

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:14.980
And I'd like to
hand it over to Joe

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:18.672
now to tell you what
TELUS is doing in detail.

00:18:18.672 --> 00:18:19.630
JOE BETTRIDGE: Perfect.

00:18:19.630 --> 00:18:21.430
Thank you for that, Chad.

00:18:21.430 --> 00:18:24.040
So just to get us started,
for those of you who are not

00:18:24.040 --> 00:18:25.930
from Canada and don't
know who TELUS is,

00:18:25.930 --> 00:18:28.540
TELUS is one of the largest
telecommunication companies

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:30.580
within Canada.

00:18:30.580 --> 00:18:32.950
So we represent about
9 and 1/2 million

00:18:32.950 --> 00:18:36.720
of the 35 million Canadians
on their cellular plans.

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:42.140
And one of the things we've
been doing for the last while is

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:44.720
we've been developing an
insights product that allows us

00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:48.380
to use massive amounts of
deidentified aggregate data

00:18:48.380 --> 00:18:51.583
to answer really complex
questions about the real world,

00:18:51.583 --> 00:18:53.000
and what we want
to announce today

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:55.730
is that we're creating an
API into this data that

00:18:55.730 --> 00:18:59.390
is launching today that
allows for users to query

00:18:59.390 --> 00:19:02.300
this data directly and
get aggregate results back

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:04.310
that have no individual
information in them

00:19:04.310 --> 00:19:07.130
but can answer really
complex business problems.

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:08.630
This is a first for
us, and it's one

00:19:08.630 --> 00:19:10.480
of the first times in
the world that an API

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:13.000
of this type of data has
been able to be generated,

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:14.490
and that's thanks
to BigQuery GIS.

00:19:16.995 --> 00:19:18.870
So the big picture of
what we're trying to do

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:21.245
is we're trying to solve the
problem that many businesses

00:19:21.245 --> 00:19:23.580
have today of making
critical decisions based

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:27.510
on gut feel, small-sample-rate
surveys, and straw-men business

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:28.590
cases.

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:31.450
We had this promise of big
data for a very long time,

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:33.540
yet we haven't seen a
lot of delivery on it.

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:36.390
We've just seen mass and mass
amounts of data be stored,

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:39.120
but no real analysis has
been capable to be completed

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:40.740
that can really
solve the questions

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:43.260
that businesses and
governments have.

00:19:43.260 --> 00:19:45.070
Usually this is for
one of two reasons.

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:47.940
Either the technology is
a roadblock or privacy

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:49.250
has been a roadblock.

00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:51.228
As I'll talk about in
the next few slides,

00:19:51.228 --> 00:19:52.770
both of these things
have been solved

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:55.350
using both BigQuery and the
previous groundwork we've

00:19:55.350 --> 00:19:57.260
laid around privacy.

00:19:57.260 --> 00:19:58.620
And we need this to be scalable.

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:00.630
This is a really critical issue.

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:02.810
Government's existing
BI tools need

00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:04.560
to be integrated and
need to be able to be

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:06.810
used within the current
supply chain of data.

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:09.330
We can't be trying to
create a new set of tools.

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:11.550
We need integration
directly to the tools today.

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:14.070
As Chad was talking to
earlier, the BigQuery GIS

00:20:14.070 --> 00:20:16.530
is really great for plugging
into a lot of different tools

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:20.050
that we commonly use.

00:20:20.050 --> 00:20:21.690
So what is TELUS Insights?

00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:24.180
TELUS Insights takes all
the aggregate information

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:26.070
being generated by
our cellular network.

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:28.980
So every time a phone
connects to a cell tower,

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:30.810
there's a residual
event that's created

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:33.990
that says this phone was at
this cell tower at this time.

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:36.420
We take all that information
across the country.

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:38.160
We remove all the identifiers.

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:40.960
We get rid of anything
that's personal from that,

00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:43.140
and we create a
row-level record saying

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:46.210
this is the data of what
happened across Canada.

00:20:46.210 --> 00:20:48.030
We then upload that
data into the cloud,

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:51.480
process it, remove a lot of the
secondary information that's

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:54.750
not necessary, and then we
start analyzing it and producing

00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:55.510
insights.

00:20:55.510 --> 00:20:57.390
These insights
could be that there

00:20:57.390 --> 00:21:00.570
were 10,000 people in the
neighborhood at a time that

00:21:00.570 --> 00:21:03.360
spent at least two days
there in the last week.

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:07.770
It could be the trade area
of a given business, maps

00:21:07.770 --> 00:21:10.090
around a certain area.

00:21:10.090 --> 00:21:12.810
The primary area of trade
is within five kilometers

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:13.500
of the business.

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:17.700
The secondary area is within
seven kilometers to the east

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:20.070
and only three
kilometers to the west.

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:23.347
We're able to pull this all
out and aggregate that through.

00:21:23.347 --> 00:21:24.930
And finally, we're
doing these results

00:21:24.930 --> 00:21:29.680
through an API that's
powered by Google Cloud.

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:31.250
In all of this, we need privacy.

00:21:31.250 --> 00:21:33.580
Privacy is a critical
cornerstone of everything

00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:36.130
we do around Insights.

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:38.830
TELUS has worked very closely
with Ann Cavoukian, who's

00:21:38.830 --> 00:21:42.030
the former privacy
commissioner of Ontario,

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:45.040
on achieving Privacy by
Design certification.

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:47.200
This certification
entails ensuring

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:51.130
that all privacy and
regulatory issues are met.

00:21:51.130 --> 00:21:54.640
The language she wrote
has influenced the GDPR

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:57.190
and has been seen
there throughout.

00:21:57.190 --> 00:22:00.650
What we do specifically is
we add in privacy controls,

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:02.090
including deidentification.

00:22:02.090 --> 00:22:05.200
So before we analyze any
data, we're deidentifying it.

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:07.090
We aggregate all
of our information

00:22:07.090 --> 00:22:08.310
into large data pools.

00:22:08.310 --> 00:22:10.810
So we don't allow anything
about individuals or even

00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:12.700
small groups of individuals.

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:14.890
Further to this, we
extrapolate all the data

00:22:14.890 --> 00:22:16.640
to match to our market share.

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:18.970
So it represents the entirety
of the country as opposed

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:21.170
to only TELUS's share.

00:22:21.170 --> 00:22:23.100
We also include
security within this.

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:24.940
We do a lot of work
on security and making

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:27.850
sure this product
is secure by design.

00:22:27.850 --> 00:22:29.650
All engagements
beyond this are also

00:22:29.650 --> 00:22:31.570
audited by our trust model.

00:22:31.570 --> 00:22:34.840
TELUS views trust as a
cornerstone of all privacy.

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:37.810
If you don't have trust,
you can't have privacy.

00:22:37.810 --> 00:22:40.640
And how we gain trust is
through being transparent.

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:42.552
So we update our
privacy policies

00:22:42.552 --> 00:22:45.010
to include what we're doing
with deidentified and aggregate

00:22:45.010 --> 00:22:45.798
data.

00:22:45.798 --> 00:22:47.590
We also understand that
not a lot of people

00:22:47.590 --> 00:22:49.390
read privacy policies,
so one of the things

00:22:49.390 --> 00:22:51.670
we've done to help this
is we've added privacy

00:22:51.670 --> 00:22:54.730
frequently asked questions
on our website that

00:22:54.730 --> 00:22:56.650
are written in very
plain and simple language

00:22:56.650 --> 00:22:59.960
that help everyone understand
exactly what we are doing.

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:01.800
And finally, we
give users control.

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:03.520
So we give users the
ability to opt out

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:05.560
of any of these
large-scale analyses

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:08.350
without asking them for
reasoning or justification.

00:23:08.350 --> 00:23:10.810
It's just a simple opt-out
if they choose to do so.

00:23:14.058 --> 00:23:15.850
With this being said,
I'm going to take you

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:17.530
through our architecture
very briefly

00:23:17.530 --> 00:23:19.780
and talk through a bit of
what we do specifically

00:23:19.780 --> 00:23:20.990
with the data.

00:23:20.990 --> 00:23:23.615
So all of this data is captured
with probes within our network.

00:23:23.615 --> 00:23:25.823
I'm not going to go into
detail about how that works.

00:23:25.823 --> 00:23:27.550
But once that data is
landed on a server,

00:23:27.550 --> 00:23:30.670
it's pushed out to
Google Cloud using NiFi.

00:23:30.670 --> 00:23:33.790
Once it lands there, it's
put into a Dataflow job,

00:23:33.790 --> 00:23:37.410
and that Dataflow job removes
all the opted-out information.

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:39.250
It sanitizes and deidentifies.

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:41.770
It strips out all the personally
identifiable information

00:23:41.770 --> 00:23:45.250
from the data, and then
it does some analysis

00:23:45.250 --> 00:23:46.397
to retain secondary traits.

00:23:46.397 --> 00:23:48.730
So that data [INAUDIBLE] could
be things like area codes

00:23:48.730 --> 00:23:50.560
or the country of origin
of the device that

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:52.750
are stored in that
personal information

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:54.875
but are not the
entirety of that data.

00:23:54.875 --> 00:23:56.500
Once all of this has
been stripped down

00:23:56.500 --> 00:23:58.240
to the skeleton of
the information,

00:23:58.240 --> 00:24:01.300
it's landed into BigQuery
where further analysis

00:24:01.300 --> 00:24:05.200
is done to remove things
like duplicate devices,

00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:06.340
undesirable devices.

00:24:06.340 --> 00:24:08.200
These might be things
like roaming devices

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:10.960
from an IoT device
that's not actually part

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:13.450
of what we're
looking to analyze.

00:24:13.450 --> 00:24:17.380
As well as we generate a
system-based universal unique

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:18.620
identifier.

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:20.782
So this identifier is
generated by the system.

00:24:20.782 --> 00:24:22.240
It's the only
identifier associated

00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:25.180
with any of the information
from this point forward,

00:24:25.180 --> 00:24:26.710
meaning there's
no tie back to any

00:24:26.710 --> 00:24:28.630
of the personal information.

00:24:28.630 --> 00:24:30.190
Once this is done,
our data is pushed

00:24:30.190 --> 00:24:31.540
through to an analysis layer.

00:24:31.540 --> 00:24:34.300
At this layer, we do a
lot of different work

00:24:34.300 --> 00:24:36.670
to reduce the amount of
data that we're storing.

00:24:36.670 --> 00:24:39.070
This includes collapsing
down each device

00:24:39.070 --> 00:24:41.900
into a timeline of what happened
to that device as opposed

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:44.030
to all the individual events.

00:24:44.030 --> 00:24:46.500
We also calculate an assumed
home and work neighborhood.

00:24:46.500 --> 00:24:48.250
We'll talk a bit more
about how that works

00:24:48.250 --> 00:24:52.340
and how we preserve privacy in
that step on the next slide.

00:24:52.340 --> 00:24:55.120
We also then include a geo
ID to each location which

00:24:55.120 --> 00:24:57.730
is used to apply market share.

00:24:57.730 --> 00:25:00.430
And finally, we compute
all of our API queries

00:25:00.430 --> 00:25:03.200
against this
aggregated data set.

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:04.910
So our final layer
is our output layer.

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:06.670
And this layer is
built in App Engine,

00:25:06.670 --> 00:25:08.830
and it allows users to
query and asks things

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:10.810
like what are the number
of visitors in a given

00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:12.952
polygon at a given time?

00:25:12.952 --> 00:25:14.410
And they get a
result returned back

00:25:14.410 --> 00:25:16.490
from that from the
analysis layer.

00:25:16.490 --> 00:25:18.550
All of these results meet
our privacy thresholds

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:20.680
and are about large
aggregate groups.

00:25:20.680 --> 00:25:22.180
We'll talk a bit
more about how this

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:25.150
is utilized in some real-world
cases in a couple slides.

00:25:27.675 --> 00:25:29.300
So the first thing
I want to talk about

00:25:29.300 --> 00:25:32.252
is our assumed home- and
work-neighborhood analysis.

00:25:32.252 --> 00:25:33.710
So the first thing
we do is we look

00:25:33.710 --> 00:25:35.390
at what tower is
a device connected

00:25:35.390 --> 00:25:36.503
to over a period of time?

00:25:36.503 --> 00:25:37.920
In this given
example, we're going

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:40.250
to look at a one-week period.

00:25:40.250 --> 00:25:44.030
In this image, we can see two
distinct clustering of towers

00:25:44.030 --> 00:25:45.660
that this device
were connected to

00:25:45.660 --> 00:25:47.660
during the time, the first
one being on the left

00:25:47.660 --> 00:25:50.250
here and the second
being on the right.

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:53.078
The one on the left has a
much higher total time spent,

00:25:53.078 --> 00:25:55.245
so we're going to look at
that cluster specifically.

00:25:55.245 --> 00:25:56.662
And there's a lot
of research that

00:25:56.662 --> 00:25:59.240
show that the time most
spent in a given area

00:25:59.240 --> 00:26:03.632
is likely that home, or the
home is likely within that area.

00:26:03.632 --> 00:26:05.090
So taking this
cluster, we're going

00:26:05.090 --> 00:26:09.020
to place a polygon around the
initial tower with the highest

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:09.800
weight.

00:26:09.800 --> 00:26:11.840
We then are going to
shuffle that polygon

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:13.670
across the different
towers to try

00:26:13.670 --> 00:26:17.660
to find a closer representation
of where the home area is.

00:26:17.660 --> 00:26:20.000
So we continue to shift this
towards the higher-weighted

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:23.030
towers until we eventually
hit that lowest-weighted tower

00:26:23.030 --> 00:26:24.470
and get a rough
estimate of where

00:26:24.470 --> 00:26:27.290
the home neighborhood would be.

00:26:27.290 --> 00:26:30.620
From here, we
overlay that polygon

00:26:30.620 --> 00:26:35.060
onto a set of polygons that are
based around Statistics Canada

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:37.550
information of where
neighborhoods are.

00:26:37.550 --> 00:26:39.050
So these are important
neighborhoods

00:26:39.050 --> 00:26:42.140
that are used in statistical
analysis of demographics

00:26:42.140 --> 00:26:44.552
and what we use for
our market share.

00:26:44.552 --> 00:26:46.010
So by applying
these neighborhoods,

00:26:46.010 --> 00:26:48.200
we use an ST_Within
function to determine

00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:50.600
exactly where that
polygon is, and then

00:26:50.600 --> 00:26:52.790
we only store that
blue polygon that

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:55.860
is much larger as the
home area for that device.

00:26:55.860 --> 00:26:57.440
So this again strips
away us storing

00:26:57.440 --> 00:26:59.660
any of this information
that's really detailed,

00:26:59.660 --> 00:27:02.090
and we go to this higher-level
piece of information

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:04.040
to just understand the
neighborhood, which is

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:05.480
used in that further analysis.

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:09.960
One of the big
projects we've had

00:27:09.960 --> 00:27:12.210
come online since
COVID-19 started

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:14.490
is our Data for Good program.

00:27:14.490 --> 00:27:17.700
Within this program, we're
providing governments as well

00:27:17.700 --> 00:27:21.240
as academic institutes
access to our information

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:23.910
at the aggregate and
deidentified layer

00:27:23.910 --> 00:27:26.760
to help them solve problems
related to COVID-19.

00:27:26.760 --> 00:27:29.490
This could be things ranging
from how health care should

00:27:29.490 --> 00:27:32.420
be impacted and what should be
done for health-care institutes

00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:34.980
through the economic
recovery as we're

00:27:34.980 --> 00:27:37.080
trying to reopen our economy.

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:38.580
We're doing this
under a strict set

00:27:38.580 --> 00:27:41.605
of rules which are outlined
on our Data for Good website.

00:27:41.605 --> 00:27:43.980
These include things like
these cannot be used for police

00:27:43.980 --> 00:27:45.270
enforcement.

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:49.560
The data cannot be used in
any way outside of the typical

00:27:49.560 --> 00:27:52.590
usages we have outlined
within our privacy policies.

00:27:52.590 --> 00:27:56.580
We will not reduce any
of our privacy thresholds

00:27:56.580 --> 00:27:57.710
for COVID-19.

00:27:57.710 --> 00:27:59.190
We are staying
strict to everything

00:27:59.190 --> 00:28:03.650
we've transparently given to
our customers going forward.

00:28:03.650 --> 00:28:05.400
But this has been a
very powerful offering

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:07.740
that many of our provinces,
federal government,

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:10.620
and several cities
are using as well

00:28:10.620 --> 00:28:12.945
to try to help in the fight
against COVID in Canada.

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:18.470
This is one specific example
of how this data can be used.

00:28:18.470 --> 00:28:20.600
This is looking at
social distancing, which

00:28:20.600 --> 00:28:23.938
was very important in the
early stages of COVID-19,

00:28:23.938 --> 00:28:26.230
really understanding where
people are leaving the home.

00:28:26.230 --> 00:28:28.840
So in these maps, you'll
see yellow as being

00:28:28.840 --> 00:28:32.470
more people than normal
being out of their home.

00:28:32.470 --> 00:28:34.332
As we head towards that
greenish-blue that's

00:28:34.332 --> 00:28:36.040
in the middle of the
scale at the bottom,

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:39.010
that's typical
behavior pre-COVID-19.

00:28:39.010 --> 00:28:43.458
As we get into the dark purples,
that is less movement behavior.

00:28:43.458 --> 00:28:45.250
Now, I want to zoom in
on one specific area

00:28:45.250 --> 00:28:48.465
to show a good example of
how this data can be used.

00:28:48.465 --> 00:28:49.840
So as we're zooming
in here, this

00:28:49.840 --> 00:28:52.052
is the area surrounding
Toronto, which

00:28:52.052 --> 00:28:54.010
is the capital of Ontario,
which is the largest

00:28:54.010 --> 00:28:55.630
province within Canada.

00:28:55.630 --> 00:28:58.480
To the north of the map, you
can see these areas lighting up

00:28:58.480 --> 00:28:59.050
in yellow.

00:28:59.050 --> 00:29:00.940
This is a very
remote and rural set

00:29:00.940 --> 00:29:04.180
of communities that are very
popular for cottage goers.

00:29:04.180 --> 00:29:05.680
And one of the
things we immediately

00:29:05.680 --> 00:29:07.472
started finding in the
data is that there's

00:29:07.472 --> 00:29:09.430
a large volume of
people traveling

00:29:09.430 --> 00:29:13.210
to these areas
from the big cities

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:15.850
to try to get away
from where crowds were.

00:29:15.850 --> 00:29:17.680
The concern here is
it puts a real strain

00:29:17.680 --> 00:29:19.570
on hospitals in these
areas that may only

00:29:19.570 --> 00:29:23.045
have one or two ventilators
available at any given time.

00:29:23.045 --> 00:29:25.420
And if there were to be an
outbreak in these communities,

00:29:25.420 --> 00:29:27.100
it would affect
them much stronger

00:29:27.100 --> 00:29:29.890
than it would a larger
city that has more ability

00:29:29.890 --> 00:29:32.660
to handle within their
health-care system.

00:29:32.660 --> 00:29:34.390
So this was being
used to understand

00:29:34.390 --> 00:29:37.180
where to redistribute
PPE in order

00:29:37.180 --> 00:29:41.210
to really help in
fighting COVID.

00:29:41.210 --> 00:29:43.670
Another way of looking at
this is the public-health lens

00:29:43.670 --> 00:29:45.710
and trying to understand
which communities

00:29:45.710 --> 00:29:48.050
are interconnected so that
if an outbreak happens in one

00:29:48.050 --> 00:29:51.290
community, you know which
other communities are likely

00:29:51.290 --> 00:29:52.740
going to be affected.

00:29:52.740 --> 00:29:55.340
So by looking at large
aggregates of how people travel

00:29:55.340 --> 00:29:57.530
from one city to another,
we're able to start

00:29:57.530 --> 00:30:00.560
determining linkages and how
closely linked different cities

00:30:00.560 --> 00:30:01.330
are.

00:30:01.330 --> 00:30:03.890
So this is looking at
three steps from Vancouver.

00:30:03.890 --> 00:30:06.710
So this is taking Vancouver and
then going up to three steps

00:30:06.710 --> 00:30:08.540
away and showing the
interconnectedness

00:30:08.540 --> 00:30:10.040
of communities.

00:30:10.040 --> 00:30:13.040
This can be used, say, if there
was an outbreak in Vancouver,

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:15.920
how long would it take to spread
to somewhere like Parksville

00:30:15.920 --> 00:30:19.160
that you see at the northeast
side of the-- sorry, northwest

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:20.237
side of the map?

00:30:20.237 --> 00:30:22.070
Or how long it would
take to spread to Hope?

00:30:22.070 --> 00:30:24.408
What's the path of
travel for that,

00:30:24.408 --> 00:30:26.450
and where would you see
other outbreaks happening

00:30:26.450 --> 00:30:28.342
before you see it there?

00:30:28.342 --> 00:30:30.050
And this can be really
critical in trying

00:30:30.050 --> 00:30:32.585
to figure out how to stop
the spread of the disease.

00:30:37.040 --> 00:30:39.040
So there's a lot of
numbers behind these maps,

00:30:39.040 --> 00:30:41.350
and BigQuery is what
really makes this possible.

00:30:41.350 --> 00:30:44.920
So we have 74 billion
records per month coming in.

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:48.250
We have 275,000 unique
points mapped out

00:30:48.250 --> 00:30:51.520
to over 5,000 unique
polygons across the country.

00:30:51.520 --> 00:30:54.787
There's 522 days of data
being used to analyze this.

00:30:54.787 --> 00:30:56.620
This includes things
like building baselines

00:30:56.620 --> 00:30:59.350
from previous years,
understanding the existing data

00:30:59.350 --> 00:31:02.140
today, and going far beyond
that to try to predict what

00:31:02.140 --> 00:31:04.250
might happen in the future.

00:31:04.250 --> 00:31:06.310
And finally, there's
1.2 petabytes

00:31:06.310 --> 00:31:07.630
of data being analyzed.

00:31:07.630 --> 00:31:10.310
This is a massive,
massive amount of data.

00:31:10.310 --> 00:31:13.090
And we're able to do this 25
times faster than we could

00:31:13.090 --> 00:31:15.460
with our previous
Hadoop infrastructure,

00:31:15.460 --> 00:31:17.360
and that's really the
power of Google Cloud

00:31:17.360 --> 00:31:19.180
here is we're able
to do this just

00:31:19.180 --> 00:31:21.038
because we have BigQuery GIS.

00:31:21.038 --> 00:31:23.080
We couldn't do this with
our previous technology,

00:31:23.080 --> 00:31:24.663
and we wouldn't have
been able to help

00:31:24.663 --> 00:31:27.520
in the fight against COVID-19
without having the access

00:31:27.520 --> 00:31:28.750
to this kind of technology.

00:31:31.760 --> 00:31:34.700
And again, today we're
announcing our TELUS Insights

00:31:34.700 --> 00:31:37.250
API which allows Canadian
companies to get access

00:31:37.250 --> 00:31:41.210
to this type of information
through a queryable API where

00:31:41.210 --> 00:31:44.720
they can build out their own
reports and analysis of what's

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:46.960
happening across Canada.

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:48.720
And to get more
information on this,

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:51.440
you can contact myself or the
TELUS Insights team through

00:31:51.440 --> 00:31:54.575
our website telus.com/insights.

00:31:54.575 --> 00:31:55.700
And back over to you, Chad.

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:00.630
CHAD JENNINGS: So, Joe,
thanks for joining me

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:01.890
in the presentation.

00:32:01.890 --> 00:32:05.700
Thanks for announcing the
launch of TELUS Insights

00:32:05.700 --> 00:32:06.750
in the presentation.

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:10.110
And also thanks for
the work you're doing.

00:32:10.110 --> 00:32:12.270
It's just a really
great example of how

00:32:12.270 --> 00:32:14.820
we can bring these
tools and this data

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:19.950
together to do good stuff
for people that need--

00:32:19.950 --> 00:32:21.600
people that need
to understand this,

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:23.853
like public-policy
decision makers.

00:32:23.853 --> 00:32:25.020
So I really appreciate that.

00:32:27.530 --> 00:32:30.080
So to wrap up, everyone--

00:32:30.080 --> 00:32:31.970
by the way, this
is the last slide.

00:32:31.970 --> 00:32:35.960
So if you're tired, we're
just about there, so hang on.

00:32:35.960 --> 00:32:40.010
But to wrap up, I wanted to talk
a little bit about the context

00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:42.920
that we have developed
here at Google

00:32:42.920 --> 00:32:44.870
around geospatial
analytics and how

00:32:44.870 --> 00:32:47.240
we're thinking about all
the different areas of it.

00:32:47.240 --> 00:32:50.030
You can tell a little
bit about this.

00:32:50.030 --> 00:32:52.440
We foreshadowed it with
the rest of the talk

00:32:52.440 --> 00:32:53.810
because we talked about imagery.

00:32:53.810 --> 00:32:55.640
We talked about tabular.

00:32:55.640 --> 00:32:58.160
So I just wanted to wrap
that all up with one slide.

00:32:58.160 --> 00:33:00.080
On the left side,
you see the types

00:33:00.080 --> 00:33:03.860
of data that frequently go
into geospatial analyses--

00:33:03.860 --> 00:33:05.330
tabular imagery.

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:08.540
On the bottom here, there
are preformed data assets--

00:33:08.540 --> 00:33:11.300
the Earth Engine catalog,
the Descartes Lab catalog,

00:33:11.300 --> 00:33:13.940
BigQuery's own
public data catalog.

00:33:13.940 --> 00:33:15.800
There are bunch
of assets that we

00:33:15.800 --> 00:33:19.100
have just available for
folks to start working on.

00:33:19.100 --> 00:33:21.770
In the center here, these
are the capabilities

00:33:21.770 --> 00:33:25.490
that a wide-ranging
geospatial-analytics

00:33:25.490 --> 00:33:26.648
practitioner will need.

00:33:26.648 --> 00:33:27.940
They have to work on tabulated.

00:33:27.940 --> 00:33:29.810
They have to work on imagery.

00:33:29.810 --> 00:33:31.760
Maybe your use case
doesn't use one of those,

00:33:31.760 --> 00:33:35.390
but we need to build it
out for those who do.

00:33:35.390 --> 00:33:37.360
The bottom ones here,
mapping and data

00:33:37.360 --> 00:33:39.110
visualization-- you
need to be able to get

00:33:39.110 --> 00:33:40.190
your results on a map.

00:33:40.190 --> 00:33:42.170
Otherwise they're
kind of meaningless.

00:33:42.170 --> 00:33:44.180
And we need to be
able to bring to bear

00:33:44.180 --> 00:33:47.600
machine learning and artificial
intelligence into this

00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:50.600
so that we can do
predictive analyses as well.

00:33:50.600 --> 00:33:52.610
Once you bring all
of those capabilities

00:33:52.610 --> 00:33:55.670
together over these data
assets and types of data

00:33:55.670 --> 00:33:57.410
that are shown here
on the left, then you

00:33:57.410 --> 00:33:59.100
get all of these use cases--

00:33:59.100 --> 00:34:01.910
so the ones that I described
earlier, the ones like Joe

00:34:01.910 --> 00:34:03.660
described.

00:34:03.660 --> 00:34:05.540
And it really is
our thesis here,

00:34:05.540 --> 00:34:07.730
and we're seeing
this being borne out,

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:10.010
which is really
exciting, that once you

00:34:10.010 --> 00:34:12.739
have these capabilities
available at the scale

00:34:12.739 --> 00:34:16.159
that they're available at
inside of Google Cloud,

00:34:16.159 --> 00:34:19.850
you can do your analyses
over much larger data sets

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:24.380
and do much more interesting
and ultimately impactful

00:34:24.380 --> 00:34:26.840
analysis for your users,
for your customers,

00:34:26.840 --> 00:34:28.310
or for the public.

00:34:28.310 --> 00:34:32.630
So with that, thank you very
much for spending a bit of time

00:34:32.630 --> 00:34:33.570
with us.

